 "transcript": {
  "answer": "The RBA has been **raising** rates in 2026, after a cut cycle in 2025.\n\n- **Cash rate target:** The monthly average was **4.36% in September 2026**, the latest data. It has been about 4.35% since June 2026.\n- **Direction:** It rose from **3.60% in January 2026** to 4.35% by June, a total of about 0.75 percentage points. The biggest monthly increases were in February through May.\n- **Context:** In 2025 the cash rate fell from 4.35% in January to 3.60% by September, then held there until the reversal in February 2026.\n\nBorrowers are seeing the increase in advertised rates:\n\n- **Standard variable home loan (owner-occupier, banks):** 8.77% in September 2026, up from 8.27% in June 2025 and 8.02% at the low point in late 2025.\n- **3-year fixed (owner-occupier):** 7.02% in September 2026, up from 5.52% in late 2025. It rose sharply in March and April 2026 and jumped again in September.\n\nThe data gives monthly averages, so I can't confirm the exact dates of each RBA decision or whether the target moved in October. Check the RBA website for its latest decision and statement.",
